[{:db/id 17592186327819 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Evaluate the improper integral:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For $R>4$, the partial-fraction identity gives\n\n$$\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx=\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}.$$\n\nBoth terms have the same finite upper bound. Integrating and combining the logarithms before taking a limit yields\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n&=\\left[\\ln(x-3)-\\ln(2x+1)\\right]_4^R\\\\\n&=\\ln\\frac{R-3}{2R+1}+\\ln9\n=\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}\n=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe limit is finite, so the improper integral converges. The two partial-fraction improper integrals each diverge when considered separately; keeping a common finite upper bound avoids an undefined subtraction." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
